\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Predicting held-out attack success from base-checkpoint diagnostics under four separations. Counts are the valid instances of that regime, which differ because each separation revalidates its own inputs; the fully separated block uses the subset for which an independent outcome-state realization was successfully reconstructed, and its class counts use the fourth judge, against \(1{,}521\) positive and \(2{,}225\) negative for HarmBench on that same subset. Intervals are \(95\%\) bootstrap, clustered on prompts. Figure~\ref {fig:diagnostics}a plots the AUROC column. The \(+0.131\) fully separated gain of Section~\ref {sec:results-diagnostic} is \(0.779\) against \(0.648\), of which \(+0.088\) is already reached by the uniform mean state margin.}}{22}{table.caption.57}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:diagnostics}{{12}{22}{Predicting held-out attack success from base-checkpoint diagnostics under four separations. Counts are the valid instances of that regime, which differ because each separation revalidates its own inputs; the fully separated block uses the subset for which an independent outcome-state realization was successfully reconstructed, and its class counts use the fourth judge, against \(1{,}521\) positive and \(2{,}225\) negative for HarmBench on that same subset. Intervals are \(95\%\) bootstrap, clustered on prompts. Figure~\ref {fig:diagnostics}a plots the AUROC column. The \(+0.131\) fully separated gain of Section~\ref {sec:results-diagnostic} is \(0.779\) against \(0.648\), of which \(+0.088\) is already reached by the uniform mean state margin}{table.caption.57}{}}
