\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Complete prompt for the fourth judge. The prose instructions are set in roman and reproduced word for word; the JSON schema and the field template, whose line structure the prompt relies on, are verbatim. The four braced fields are substituted per instance. \texttt  {recovery\_onset\_token} is one-based in the evaluated checkpoint's continuation-token sequence, excluding chat-template and special tokens; \texttt  {null}, an out-of-range index, or malformed output is treated conservatively as no observed recovery. Recovery within \(64\) tokens means an onset index at most \(64\); conditional TTR uses the same index over the full \(256\)-token window. The rubric fixes the \(0\)--\(3\) severity scale of Appendix~\ref {app:recovery-quality}.}}{24}{figure.caption.66}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:judge-prompt}{{5}{24}{Complete prompt for the fourth judge. The prose instructions are set in roman and reproduced word for word; the JSON schema and the field template, whose line structure the prompt relies on, are verbatim. The four braced fields are substituted per instance. \texttt {recovery\_onset\_token} is one-based in the evaluated checkpoint's continuation-token sequence, excluding chat-template and special tokens; \texttt {null}, an out-of-range index, or malformed output is treated conservatively as no observed recovery. Recovery within \(64\) tokens means an onset index at most \(64\); conditional TTR uses the same index over the full \(256\)-token window. The rubric fixes the \(0\)--\(3\) severity scale of Appendix~\ref {app:recovery-quality}}{figure.caption.66}{}}
